<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Codex Amiatinus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Codex Amiatinus is the oldest surviving complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Vulgate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. It was made at the twin monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey">Wearmouth-Jarrow</a> in Northumbria in the early 8th century, one of three great Bibles commissioned by Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceolfrith">Ceolfrith</a>, who took it towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a gift for the pope in 716. It is now in the Laurentian Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and takes its name from the abbey of Monte Amiata.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codex-amiatinus.jpg" alt="Codex Amiatinus" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CodxAmiatinusFolio5rEzra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is a single immense volume about 49 cm high, 34 cm wide and 18 cm thick, weighing over 34 kg. It holds 1,040 leaves of vellum arranged in quires of four sheets, written in large, regular uncial script in two columns of 43 or 44 lines. There is no punctuation; the text is set out instead in sense lines, cola et commata, a layout thought to be modelled on the Codex Grandior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassiodorus">Cassiodorus</a>, which the monastery owned. Its two full-page miniatures show little of the Insular style of Northumbrian art and were copied from late antique originals.</p><p>Designated A or am in critical editions, it is commonly considered the most reliable witness to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a>'s Vulgate for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> and most of the Old. The Psalms are given in Jerome's translation from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, though in a weaker text taken from an Irish psalter. As in all Vulgate Bibles before the 9th century, the Book of Baruch and the Letter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> are absent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceolfrith">Ceolfrith</a> commissioned the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bibles</a> in 692, a date fixed by the monastery's grant of extra land to raise the 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> needed for the vellum, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> was most likely involved in the work. After Ceolfrith died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> on 29 September 716, the book reappears in the 9th century at the Abbazia di San Salvatore on Monte Amiata, where a list of relics of 1036 described it as written in the hand of Pope Gregory. It passed to the Laurentian Library in 1786.</p><p>Its dedication page had been altered, and the librarian Angelo Maria Bandini attributed it to Servandus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> around the 540s, a claim accepted for a century. In 1888 Giovanni Battista de Rossi showed that it was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bibles</a> mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> the codex mattered to Catholics because its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text was older than any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> manuscript then known. In 1587 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-v">Pope Sixtus V</a> had it sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for the Sixtine Vulgate, though its editors made little or no use of its readings. It became a primary source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> critical edition of the Vulgate New Testament proposed by John Wordsworth and published between 1889 and 1954, and of the Benedictine edition commissioned by Pope Pius X in 1907, both of which took their layout from it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Codex Amiatinus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Codex Amiatinus</b>, made at Wearmouth-Jarrow and the oldest surviving complete Latin Bible, was long thought to be Italian until its Northumbrian origin was identified in the 1880s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
